#!/usr/bin/env python3
from pathlib import Path
import json,html,re
HERE=Path(__file__).resolve().parent;ROOT=HERE.parents[1]
def tex(s,display=False):
 return '<div class="math" data-display="true" data-tex="'+html.escape(s,quote=True)+'"></div>' if display else '<span class="math" data-tex="'+html.escape(s,quote=True)+'"></span>'
def main():
 space=json.loads((HERE/'basis.json').read_text());report=json.loads((HERE/'verification.json').read_text());assert report['passed']
 css=re.search(r'<style>(.*?)</style>',(ROOT/'research/t2-spin2-inertia/index.html').read_text(),re.S).group(1)
 body='''<p class="eyebrow">Anticoherent subspaces · Exact algebraic construction</p><a class="back-link" href="../../index.html#n=20&amp;t=2&amp;subspace=algebraic-n20-k4-t2">← Return to the catalogue</a><h1>Four dimensions at twenty qubits</h1><p class="lead">An exact rational existence certificate turns a refined numerical example into a proved four-dimensional second-order AC subspace. The matching upper bound establishes optimality.</p><div class="result"><strong>Proved maximum</strong>'''+tex(r'\boxed{K(20,2)=4}',True)+'''<p>The coefficients are explicitly defined real algebraic numbers. Their decimal approximations are supplied for visualization; no short radical formula is claimed.</p></div>
<h2>An explicit algebraic basis</h2><p>Use normalized Dicke states and set '''+tex(r'\epsilon=(1,1,0,0)')+'''. The four orthonormal basis vectors are</p>'''+tex(r'|u_a\rangle=\sum_{\substack{0\le r\le20\\r\equiv\epsilon_a\;\mathrm{mod}\;2}}\sqrt{\binom{20}{r}}\,(a_{ar}+ib_{ar})|D_{20}^{(r)}\rangle,\qquad a=1,2,3,4.',True)+'''<p>Order the 84 real coordinates by basis vector, Dicke index, and then real and imaginary part. Twelve coordinates are fixed exact rational numbers. The remaining 72 are the unique common zero of the explicitly listed quadratic equations in the explicitly listed rational box. <a href="basis.json">Download every Dicke coefficient, equation and parameter interval ↓</a>.</p><p><a href="../../index.html#n=20&amp;t=2&amp;subspace=algebraic-n20-k4-t2">Inspect the basis and its four Majorana spheres ↗</a>. The sphere coordinates are numerical renderings; their root multiplicities have separate exact interval certificates.</p>
<h2>Every cross condition is included</h2><p>For '''+tex(r'B=(u_1,u_2,u_3,u_4)')+''', the equations impose</p>'''+tex(r'\begin{aligned}B^\dagger B&=I_4,&B^\dagger J_zB&=0,&B^\dagger(3J_z^2-110I)B&=0,\\B^\dagger J_+B&=0,&B^\dagger J_+J_zB&=0,&B^\dagger J_+^2B&=0.\end{aligned}',True)+'''<p>Parity removes identically zero entries. Hermitian conjugation removes redundant entries from the first three matrices. Exactly 72 real equations remain, and all are included. They imply the complete ordered cross reductions</p>'''+tex(r'\operatorname{Tr}_{18}(|u_a\rangle\langle u_b|)=\delta_{ab}\frac{I_{\mathrm{Sym}^2}}3,\qquad 1\le a,b\le4.',True)+'''<p>Thus every complex superposition in the four-dimensional span is second-order anticoherent.</p>
<h2>Existence from exact rational inequalities</h2><p>Write the full system as '''+tex(r'F:\mathbb R^{72}\to\mathbb R^{72}')+'''. The certificate supplies a rational center '''+tex('c')+''', a rational matrix '''+tex('A')+''' and radius '''+tex(r'r=10^{-35}')+'''. For '''+tex(r'G(x)=x-AF(x)')+''', exact integer arithmetic proves</p>'''+tex(r'\begin{aligned}\|I-AJ_F(c)\|_\infty&<4\cdot10^{-45},\\\|AF(c)\|_\infty&<5\cdot10^{-61},\\\sup_{\|x-c\|_\infty\le r}\|DG(x)\|_\infty&<3\cdot10^{-27},\\\|G(c)-c\|_\infty+r\sup\|DG\|_\infty&<5.3\cdot10^{-61}<r.\end{aligned}',True)+'''<p>Therefore '''+tex('G')+''' is a strict contraction of the closed rational box into itself. It has a unique fixed point there. The inverse-defect inequality also makes '''+tex('A')+''' invertible, so that fixed point satisfies '''+tex('F(x)=0')+'''. The isolated nonsingular root has algebraic coordinates.</p><p>The primary verifier reconstructs every physical moment equation and recomputes these bounds using only integers and rational fractions. A separate verifier derives the equations independently from spin matrices and checks the same certificate.</p>
<h2>The matching upper bound</h2>'''+tex(r'4k\le N-2=18\quad\Longrightarrow\quad k\le4.',True)+'''<p>The <a href="../ancilla-inertia/index.html">auxiliary spin-3/2 theorem</a> supplies the upper bound. The algebraic construction supplies dimension four, proving equality.</p>
<h2>Proof, coefficients and checks</h2><p><a href="proof.tex">Full LaTeX proof ↓</a> · <a href="basis.json">Algebraic basis ↓</a> · <a href="certificate.json">Rational certificate ↓</a> · <a href="verify.py">Portable verifier ↓</a> · <a href="verify_independently.py">Independent verifier ↓</a> · <a href="independent-verification.json">Independent report ↓</a> · <a href="geometry-verification.json">Majorana root certificate ↓</a></p><p>The numerical seed comes from the supplied Julia subspace '''+tex(r'\Pi_{20,4,2}')+'''. Refinement suggested the certificate center. The rational existence proof establishes the exact result; equality to the original decimal span is not asserted.</p><footer><a href="../t2-spin2-inertia/index.html">General second-order bounds and remaining open cases ↗</a></footer>'''
 head='<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Twenty-qubit second-order AC subspaces: exact maximum four</title><link rel="stylesheet" href="../../vendor/katex/katex.min.css"><script defer src="../../vendor/katex/katex.min.js"></script><style>'+css+'</style><script>document.addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded",()=>document.querySelectorAll("[data-tex]").forEach(el=>{if(window.katex)el.innerHTML=katex.renderToString(el.dataset.tex,{displayMode:el.dataset.display==="true",throwOnError:false,strict:"ignore",trust:false});}));</script></head><body><main>'
 (HERE/'index.html').write_text(head+body+'</main></body></html>\n')
if __name__=='__main__':main()
